<%#
  A labelled menu button: one trigger that names what is inside it, and a panel of related actions.

    render "shared/essentials/menu_button",
      label: "Export", icon: "bi-box-arrow-up", items: [
        {label: "Requests as CSV", path: requests_path(format: :csv), icon: "bi-filetype-csv"},
        {label: "Unfulfilled picklists, PDF", path: …, icon: "bi-printer"}
      ]

  Locals:
    label    required; the visible label, and the accessible name of the trigger
    items    required; see `menu_items`
    icon     optional; a Bootstrap Icons class for the trigger
    variant  optional; defaults to :secondary
    size     optional; defaults to :md, so it lines up with the other page header actions
    align    optional; :end (default) or :start, for which edge the panel hangs from

  This is the page-level counterpart to `row_actions`, which is a kebab because a table row has no
  space for a word. A page header does, and a named menu beats an unnamed one: "Export" says what is
  inside, "More actions" only says that something is. See design.md.

  Counts as **one** action against the page header's limit of three -- design.md's own remedy for a
  fourth button is to collapse the least-used behind a menu.
%>
<%
  variant ||= :secondary
  size ||= :md
  icon ||= nil
  align ||= :end
  items = items.compact
%>
<%#
  One item is not a menu. Both of these are conditional on the data -- the picklist only exists
  while something is unfulfilled -- so an organisation with requests but none outstanding got a
  menu containing a single entry, which is strictly worse than the button it replaced: a click to
  reach one thing, with its label hidden behind a general word.

  It collapses to the *menu's* label rather than the item's, because a menu here is named after its
  contents -- see design.md -- so its name is a fair name for any one of them. It is also the verb:
  "Export" says what will happen, where "Requests as CSV" is a noun phrase. Same as GitHub's
  Download menu, which is a plain "Download" button when there is only one thing to download.
%>
<% if items.one? %>
  <% only = items.first %>
  <% if only[:method] %>
    <%= essentials_action_button label, only[:path], method: only[:method], variant: variant,
          size: size, icon: icon || only[:icon], confirm: only[:confirm] %>
  <% else %>
    <%= essentials_link_button label, only[:path], variant: variant, size: size,
          icon: icon || only[:icon], **(only[:link_options] || {}) %>
  <% end %>
<% else %>
<%#
  Renders through `<rfg-menu>` (vendor/javascript/rfg-menu.js -- designsystem's ADR 14), the same
  component `row_actions` uses -- see that partial's own comment for why this replaced
  `popover_controller`'s hand-built `role="menu"` markup. `align` maps onto the component's own
  `placement` property (`@floating-ui/dom` under the hood) rather than a `left-0`/`right-0` class on
  a hand-positioned panel -- it also now clamps to the viewport generically, which is what the old
  comment about a negative x at 320px was working around by hand.
%>
<%= content_tag("rfg-menu", label: label, placement: (align == :start ? "bottom-start" : "bottom-end")) do %>
  <button type="button" slot="trigger"
          class="<%= essentials_button_classes(variant: variant, size: size) %>">
    <% if icon %><i class="<%= icon %>" aria-hidden="true"></i><% end %>
    <%= label %>
    <%# Decorative: the chevron says "this opens", which `aria-haspopup` already tells a screen
        reader, so repeating it in the name would be noise. %>
    <i class="bi-chevron-down text-xs" aria-hidden="true"></i>
  </button>

  <%= render "shared/essentials/menu_items", items: items %>
<% end %>
<% end %>
